nt/stable reinforcement learning VLA, Multimodal signals, such as speech Hallucination Use Self Verification Use Mechanistic Interpretability Use Open Ended/Open World Models for exploration Agent Behavior Scalable simulation infrastructure, role playing Distill reasoning to small models Models of agent and human behavior, emergent behavior, future of society Self reflection In-model self reflection and metareasoning Metacognition, personality Automated Scientific Discovery Safety Assistants: Responsibility, liability Privacy, fairness of data Wider application areas for assistants Table 4. Summary of Research Agenda for Agentic LLM'。p52 结论：三类能力都受益于 reasoning 与 self-verification → shared_foundations = [reasoning, self_verification]；原文 'and all categories benefit from reasoning and self-verification.'。p52 还指出 acting/interacting 为预训练与微调生成训练数据，形成循环。\", evidence_ids=['ev-99f0f0a37d328bfed1a9', 'ev-01ce8948bcc88d235ea3'], section='outlook')\nr2 = read_pdf(start_page=12, end_page=13)\nprint(r1)\nprint(r2)\n```", "code_action": "r1 = write_note(title='outlook', content=\"5 Outlook（p50-52）：Table 4（p51）列出五个研究议题：Training Data、Hallucination、Agent Behavior、Self reflection、Safety → agenda_topics = [training_data, hallucination, agent_behavior, self_reflection, safety]。p51 原文连续表区：'Topic Challenge Training Data Finetune with inference time reasoning data Convergent/stable reinforcement learning VLA, Multimodal signals, such as speech Hallucination Use Self Verification Use Mechanistic Interpretability Use Open Ended/Open World Models for exploration Agent Behavior Scalable simulation infrastructure, role playing Distill reasoning to small models Models of agent and human behavior, emergent behavior, future of society Self reflection In-model self reflection and metareasoning Metacognition, personality Automated Scientific Discovery Safety Assistants: Responsibility, liability Privacy, fairness of data Wider application areas for assistants Table 4. Summary of Research Agenda for Agentic LLM'。p52 结论：三类能力都受益于 reasoning 与 self-verification → shared_foundations = [reasoning, self_verification]；原文 'and all categories benefit from reasoning and self-verification.'。p52 还指出 acting/interacting 为预训练与微调生成训练数据，形成循环。\", evidence_ids=['ev-99f0f0a37d328bfed1a9', 'ev-01ce8948bcc88d235ea3'], section='outlook')\nr2 = read_pdf(start_page=12, end_page=13)\nprint(r1)\nprint(r2)", "observations": "Execution logs:\n{'note_id': 'outlook', 'sources': ['paper.pdf'], 'chars': 1147}\n{'argv': ['/mnt/d/Muiti-Agent Programing/experiments/code-agent-smolagents/24371220/.venv/bin/python', 'read_pdf.py', '--start-page', '12', '--end-page', '13'], 'cwd': '/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/stability-paper-02-o5gu4i0w/results/paper/repo', 'rc': 0, 'stdout': '{\"pdf\": \"/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/stability-paper-02-o5gu4i0w/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf\", \"pages\": [{\"page\": 12, \"text\": \"1:12   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nFig. 4. Chain of Though Prompting. In blue at the top the prompt, in green at the bottom the answer. When shown the\\\\nlonger example prompt—the chain of thought—the LLM follows the steps when answering the question [Wei et al., 2022b].\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n                    Fig. 5. Comparison of PDDL and natural language for Blocksworld [Bohnet et al., 2024]\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nthe problem specification). LLaMEA (Large Language Model Evolutionary Algorithm) leverages evolutionary\\\\ncomputation methods to generate and optimize evolutionary algorithms [van Stein and Bäck, 2024].\\\\n  Planners are also combined with LLMs at the language level. Bohnet et al. [2024] provide a benchmark for\\\\nPDDL [Aeronautiques et al., 1998] based planning problems. They study how LLMs can achieve success in the\\\\nplanning domain (Figure 5).\\\\n  In Section 2.3 on retrieval augmentation, we will see further approaches where deep learning and symbolic\\\\napproaches are successfully combined [Gao et al., 2023b].\\\\n2.1.3 Search Tree. Chain of Thought uses a prompt that causes the model to perform a sequence of steps. When\\\\nthere is a single next step, that will be taken. When there are more possibilities, it is unclear how the next step\\\\n\\\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\\\n\"}, {\"page\": 13, \"text\": \"                                                                                                               Agentic LLM     •   1:13\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n\\\\n       Fig. 6. Reasoning structure of Chain-of-Thought, Self-Consistency, and Tree-of-Thoughts [Yao et al., 2024]\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nshould be selected. A greedy method selects the single step that looks best, follows only that step, and forgets the\\\\nalternatives (Chain of Thought). Ideally, we should follow the tree of all possible steps. This method is chosen in\\\\nthe Tree of Thoughts approach [Yao et al., 2024]. Here, an external control algorithm is created, that calls the\\\\nmodel, each time with a different prompt, so that it follows a tree of reasoning steps. When one reasoning path\\\\nhas been traversed, the search backtracks, and tries an alternative. The paper describes both a breadth-first and a\\\\ndepth-first controller.\\\\n   Together, the trio that consists of a generation prompt, an evaluation prompt, and an external search algorithm,\\\\nallows a systematic tree-shaped exploration of the space of reasoning steps. Figure 6 illustrates the different\\\\nreasoning structures. (Another approach, Graph of Thoughts, allows even more complex relations between the\\\\nreasoning steps [Besta et al., 2024].)\\\\n   Many works introduce variants on external prompt-improvement loops, to have explicit control over the\\\\nreasoning process. They use techniques from planning and tree search [Hart et al., 1968, Plaat, 1996] to be able to\\\\nuse backtracking to traverse the space of possible combinations of reasoning steps [Yao et al., 2024, Xie et al.,\\\\n2024, Besta et al., 2024, Schultz et al., 2024, Browne et al., 2012, Gandhi et al., 2024]. Other methods are also\\\\nused for prompt creation. Evolutionary algorithms [Romera-Paredes et al., 2024, van Stein and Bäck, 2024] and\\\\nplanning methods [Bohnet et al., 2024, Valmeekam et al., 2023, Kambhampati et al., 2024] are used to create new\\\\nprompts and heuristic algorithms for LLMs, and, synergistically, to use LLMs to create new heuristic evolutionary\\\\nand planning algorithms.\\\\n   The external search algorithm can also be used to generate training data, for finetuning the LLM, or for\\\\npretraining. In this way, we can try to see if an LLM can be taught to search possible steps implicitly, without\\\\nthe need for an external control loop. In the Stream of Search approach Gandhi et al. [2024] create a language\\\\nfor search sequences, and subsequently train an LLM on search trees that contain both good and bad outcomes,\\\\nimproving the accuracy of the model. This approach internalizes the outcome of external searches into the\\\\nLLM. Schultz et al. [2024] further show how such search results can be used to train an LLM and achieve\\\\nGrandmaster-level performance in Chess, Connect Four, and Hex.\\\\n\\\\n2.2   Self Reflection\\\\nReasoning methods draw inspiration from step-by-step human solution approaches. The more elaborate ap-\\\\nproaches use explicit planning-like methods to look ahead and use feedback for verification. These methods use a\\\\n\\\\n                                       Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\\\n\"}]}\\n', 'stderr': '', 'timed_out': False, 'pdf': '/home/ventsdenye/.local/state/code-agent/evaluations/stability-paper-02-o5gu4i0w/results/paper/repo/paper.pdf', 'pages': [{'page': 12, 'text': '1:12   •   Plaat, Van Duijn, Van Stein, Preuss, Van der Putten & Batenburg\\n\\n\\n\\n\\nFig. 4. Chain of Though Prompting. In blue at the top the prompt, in green at the bottom the answer. When shown the\\nlonger example prompt—the chain of thought—the LLM follows the steps when answering the question [Wei et al., 2022b].\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n                    Fig. 5. Comparison of PDDL and natural language for Blocksworld [Bohnet et al., 2024]\\n\\n\\nthe problem specification). LLaMEA (Large Language Model Evolutionary Algorithm) leverages evolutionary\\ncomputation methods to generate and optimize evolutionary algorithms [van Stein and Bäck, 2024].\\n  Planners are also combined with LLMs at the language level. Bohnet et al. [2024] provide a benchmark for\\nPDDL [Aeronautiques et al., 1998] based planning problems. They study how LLMs can achieve success in the\\nplanning domain (Figure 5).\\n  In Section 2.3 on retrieval augmentation, we will see further approaches where deep learning and symbolic\\napproaches are successfully combined [Gao et al., 2023b].\\n2.1.3 Search Tree. Chain of Thought uses a prompt that causes the model to perform a sequence of steps. When\\nthere is a single next step, that will be taken. When there are more possibilities, it is unclear how the next step\\n\\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n'}, {'page': 13, 'text': '                                                                                                               Agentic LLM     •   1:13\\n\\n\\n\\n\\n       Fig. 6. Reasoning structure of Chain-of-Thought, Self-Consistency, and Tree-of-Thoughts [Yao et al., 2024]\\n\\n\\nshould be selected. A greedy method selects the single step that looks best, follows only that step, and forgets the\\nalternatives (Chain of Thought). Ideally, we should follow the tree of all possible steps. This method is chosen in\\nthe Tree of Thoughts approach [Yao et al., 2024]. Here, an external control algorithm is created, that calls the\\nmodel, each time with a different prompt, so that it follows a tree of reasoning steps. When one reasoning path\\nhas been traversed, the search backtracks, and tries an alternative. The paper describes both a breadth-first and a\\ndepth-first controller.\\n   Together, the trio that consists of a generation prompt, an evaluation prompt, and an external search algorithm,\\nallows a systematic tree-shaped exploration of the space of reasoning steps. Figure 6 illustrates the different\\nreasoning structures. (Another approach, Graph of Thoughts, allows even more complex relations between the\\nreasoning steps [Besta et al., 2024].)\\n   Many works introduce variants on external prompt-improvement loops, to have explicit control over the\\nreasoning process. They use techniques from planning and tree search [Hart et al., 1968, Plaat, 1996] to be able to\\nuse backtracking to traverse the space of possible combinations of reasoning steps [Yao et al., 2024, Xie et al.,\\n2024, Besta et al., 2024, Schultz et al., 2024, Browne et al., 2012, Gandhi et al., 2024]. Other methods are also\\nused for prompt creation. Evolutionary algorithms [Romera-Paredes et al., 2024, van Stein and Bäck, 2024] and\\nplanning methods [Bohnet et al., 2024, Valmeekam et al., 2023, Kambhampati et al., 2024] are used to create new\\nprompts and heuristic algorithms for LLMs, and, synergistically, to use LLMs to create new heuristic evolutionary\\nand planning algorithms.\\n   The external search algorithm can also be used to generate training data, for finetuning the LLM, or for\\npretraining. In this way, we can try to see if an LLM can be taught to search possible steps implicitly, without\\nthe need for an external control loop. In the Stream of Search approach Gandhi et al. [2024] create a language\\nfor search sequences, and subsequently train an LLM on search trees that contain both good and bad outcomes,\\nimproving the accuracy of the model. This approach internalizes the outcome of external searches into the\\nLLM. Schultz et al. [2024] further show how such search results can be used to train an LLM and achieve\\nGrandmaster-level performance in Chess, Connect Four, and Hex.\\n\\n2.2   Self Reflection\\nReasoning methods draw inspiration from step-by-step human solution approaches. The more elaborate ap-\\nproaches use explicit planning-like methods to look ahead and use feedback for verification. These methods use a\\n\\n                                       Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research, Vol. 1, Article 1. Publication date: December 2025.\\n'}], 'evidence_ids': ['ev-5ee42fcee0f3b22d5203', 'ev-4049593a30e2846f8394'], 'delivery': 'Originals queued for the next model request; large batches may span requests.'}\nLast output from code snippet:\nNone", "observations_images": null, "action_output": null, "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 7579, "output_tokens": 1652, "total_tokens": 9231}, "is_final_answer": false}}
